Looking at this problem, I need to prove that in a doubly infinite array where each positive integer appears exactly 8 times, there must exist some $(m,n)$ with $a_{m,n} > mn$.

**Proof.**

Suppose for contradiction that $a_{m,n} \le mn$ for all positive integers $(m,n)$.

For a positive integer $T$, define
$$B_T = \{(m,n) \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}^2 : mn \le T\}.$$

**Claim:** $|B_T| \le 8T$ for every $T$.

*Proof of Claim.* For any $(m,n) \in B_T$, our assumption gives $a_{m,n} \le mn \le T$, so $a_{m,n} \in \{1, 2, \dots, T\}$. Since each positive integer $k$ appears exactly 8 times in the entire array, the value $k$ appears at most 8 times among the cells of $B_T$. The cells of $B_T$ are filled with values from $\{1, \dots, T\}$, each used at most 8 times, giving $|B_T| \le 8T$. $\square$

**Counting $|B_T|$ from below.** We have
$$|B_T| = \sum_{m=1}^{T} \left\lfloor \frac{T}{m} \right\rfloor \ge \sum_{m=1}^{T} \left(\frac{T}{m} - 1\right) = T\, H_T - T,$$
where $H_T = \sum_{m=1}^{T} \frac{1}{m}$ is the $T$-th harmonic number. It is well known that $H_T \ge \ln(T+1)$, so
$$|B_T| \ge T\bigl(\ln(T+1) - 1\bigr).$$

**Deriving the contradiction.** We need $T(\ln(T+1) - 1) > 8T$, i.e., $\ln(T+1) > 9$, i.e., $T + 1 > e^9$. Since $e^9 \approx 8103.08$, taking $T = 8103$ gives
$$|B_{8103}| \ge 8103 \cdot (\ln 8104 - 1) > 8103 \cdot (9.0001 - 1) = 8103 \cdot 8.0001 > 8 \cdot 8103 = 64824.$$

This contradicts the Claim that $|B_T| \le 8T$.

Therefore our assumption that $a_{m,n} \le mn$ for all $(m,n)$ is false, and there exist positive integers $(m,n)$ with $a_{m,n} > mn$. $\blacksquare$

